<header class="page-header">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow">Join us</span>
    <h1>Volunteering and internships</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">
      The EISS is run entirely by volunteers. Contribute remotely, at about a day a week over two
      months, and leave with something that carries your name: a brief published under your own
      byline, or a credited contribution to the Anthology. Open to students and to recent graduates
      alike.
    </p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>What you get out of it</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      These are what the placement owes you, not favours we extend if there is time. They are agreed
      at the start and written into your objectives.
    </p>

    <div class="tile-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); margin-block: var(--space-6);">
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("file-text") }}</div>
        <h3>Something published under your name</h3>
        <p>
          If your track produces a brief, it goes out under your own byline. It is a defined part of
          the placement rather than something we fit in at the end.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("book-open") }}</div>
        <h3>Credited by name either way</h3>
        <p>
          Two of the four tracks produce no byline. Indexing and data work are credited by name,
          with a link to what you did, on this page and in the project's changelog.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("users") }}</div>
        <h3>A conversation with the board</h3>
        <p>
          Half an hour with a board member about your own research interests, arranged early rather
          than left to drift. Access to people is the part you cannot get elsewhere.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("calendar") }}</div>
        <h3>A weekly call with the others</h3>
        <p>
          The placement is remote, so the group meets for a short call each week. Missing one is
          fine. It exists so that working alone does not mean working in silence.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2>What you would work on</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      We match this to what interests you rather than handing you a fixed job. Most volunteers take
      on one of these, and many move between them. Everything currently open is on the
      <a href="{{ site.helpWantedUrl }}">“help wanted” list</a>, and all of it is real outstanding
      work rather than an exercise.
    </p>

    <div class="tile-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); margin-block: var(--space-6);">
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("search") }}</div>
        <h3>Corpus and abstract indexing</h3>
        <p>
          Tracking down the abstracts of papers given at earlier conferences, checking them against
          the programme, and adding them to the Anthology so the record is complete.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><strong>You produce:</strong> a batch of recovered abstracts, credited to you by name.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("file-text") }}</div>
        <h3>Researching and writing a thematic brief</h3>
        <p>
          A short piece on one of the Anthology's research themes, drawn from the papers already in
          the corpus. The corpus removes the blank page: a brief on deterrence starts from 46 tagged
          papers and their authors.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><strong>You produce:</strong> a published brief under your own byline.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("settings") }}</div>
        <h3>Improving the database</h3>
        <p>
          Strengthening the data behind the Anthology: author names and affiliations, links to
          published versions, and the connections the Atlas draws between papers and people.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><strong>You produce:</strong> a reviewed dataset the site draws on, credited to you by name.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("users") }}</div>
        <h3>Annual Conference preparation</h3>
        <p>
          Helping the team get our flagship event ready, from the call for papers through to the
          programme. Part of the year for everyone here, ourselves included.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><strong>You produce:</strong> a conference that happened. Seasonal, and heaviest in spring.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>How the placement works</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      The terms, stated in advance. We would rather be plain about the limits than have you find
      them out in month two.
    </p>

    <dl class="definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>About a day a week, over two months</dt>
        <dd>
          We agree the rhythm at the start, and you can change it as your term or your job demands.
          There is no minimum you owe us and you are free to stop earlier.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Unpaid, and we say so plainly</dt>
        <dd>
          The EISS has no salaried staff. Everyone here is a volunteer, the board included. Expenses
          agreed with us beforehand are reimbursed against receipts, and nothing else is paid. What
          the placement gives back is the byline, the credit and the people, which is why they are
          stated as entitlements above.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Volunteering by default</dt>
        <dd>
          Most people join us as <em lang="fr">bénévoles</em>. There is no contract, you choose which
          track you take, you set your own pace, and you can stop at any point without giving a
          reason. That freedom is what the arrangement rests on, so we mean it rather than reciting
          it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>A <em lang="fr">convention de stage</em> where your programme needs one</dt>
        <dd>
          French law treats a placement as a <em lang="fr">stage</em> only where it sits inside a
          course carrying at least 200 hours of teaching a year. If yours does, we sign the
          tripartite convention with you and your institution and name a
          <em lang="fr">tuteur de stage</em>. Tell us early, because the paperwork goes through your
          university rather than through us.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Someone to ask, rather than someone checking up on you</dt>
        <dd>
          You get a named point of contact from your first day, and the weekly call. Between those
          two, nobody here monitors your hours or asks for a status report.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>One published output, and an editorial read before it goes up</dt>
        <dd>
          We cap the promise at one publishable output per person, because our reviewing capacity is
          the real limit rather than your willingness. Anything we publish carries the EISS name and
          a permanent address, so we read it first and we may decide not to publish a piece. That is
          a judgement about a text and nothing else, and it leaves your credit for the rest of your
          work untouched.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>The year at a glance</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      Placements starting in January suit the cycle best. The conference falls in June, which is the
      one fixed point everything else bends around.
    </p>

    <dl class="definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>January · Induction</dt>
        <dd>
          A short shared start on the corpus, the house style and the standard we publish to. You
          pick your track and we agree your single output.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>February to April · The substantive window</dt>
        <dd>
          The part we protect, because once conference preparation begins it will not happen. Track
          work, the weekly call, and a first draft due before April rather than after.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>April to June · The annual conference</dt>
        <dd>
          Logistics is heaviest here and everyone takes some of it, ourselves included. Expect the
          writing to slow down. We plan for that rather than being surprised by it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>July · Publication and credit</dt>
        <dd>
          Briefs go up, contributors are added to this page, and we ask the group what was worth
          doing and what was filler. That answer shapes the next cycle.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

